# BOOTSTRAPPED 2026-07-14, first real application of the Persona-era
# bootstrap procedure (see docs/sidecar-cleanup-handoff.md), surfaced
# via extraction/top_persons.py's entity-index worklist (519 mentions
# across 20 documents — a clear, well-evidenced recurring guest, not a
# marginal signal).
#############################################

tgs:Human.ArtBerman a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Arthur E. Berman"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Also publicly known as 'Art Berman.' Petroleum geologist, 40+ years in the oil and gas industry as of 2026 (bio pages give varying year-counts, e.g. 36/40/42/45, depending on when each was written — not a real discrepancy, just an incrementing 'years of experience' figure across different publication dates). M.S. Geology, Colorado School of Mines; B.A. History, Amherst College. Worked 20 years at Amoco (now BP), independent consulting geologist since. Associate editor, AAPG (American Association of Petroleum Geologists) Bulletin. Past Editor, Houston Geological Society Bulletin (2004-2005); past Vice-President of that Society (2008-2009). Director, ASPO-USA (Association for the Study of Peak Oil & Gas USA) — not yet modeled as its own Organization individual, deferred. Adjunct lecturer, University of Houston — not yet modeled as an AcademicInstitution relationship, deferred. Widely credited with coining the term 'energy blindness' — see thinkr:influencedBy on tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, added as Candidate-confidence pending human review, not yet Curated. NO owl:sameAs asserted: checked for a genuine Wikipedia page across two separate searches 2026-07-14, none found despite extensive independent bio coverage (Forbes, OilPrice.com, Financial Sense, Resilience.org, and thegreatsimplification.com's own episode pages) — confirmed absent, not merely unchecked. Wikidata was NOT independently checked (distinct from the Wikipedia check) — a real gap, not a confirmed absence, flag for follow-up before assuming no Wikidata entry exists either."@en ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:Organization.TheOilDrum ;
    .

#############################################
# BOOTSTRAPPED 2026-07-14 — Josh Farley, second real application of the
# updated bootstrap procedure. 232 mentions/16 documents in the entity
# index.
#############################################

tgs:Human.JoshFarley a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Joshua C. Farley"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Also publicly known as 'Josh Farley.' Ecological economist, Professor of Community Development & Applied Economics and Public Administration, University of Vermont; Fellow, Gund Institute for Environment (formerly Gund Institute for Ecological Economics — not yet modeled as its own AcademicInstitution/Organization individual, same deferred status as Berman's ASPO-USA). Past President, International Society for Ecological Economics (ISEE). BS Biology (Grinnell College), MIA (Columbia University), PhD Agricultural and Resource Economics (Cornell University). Co-author with Herman Daly of 'Ecological Economics: Principles and Applications' (Island Press, 2003; 2nd ed. 2010) — a foundational textbook in the field. Current research focus, per his own guest-intro description in TGS_185 (2026-07-15 bio-intro extraction pilot finding): economics of essential resources, democratization of monetary and financial systems, evolution of cooperation, economics of information, and the commons. NOTE: Herman Daly is himself a confirmed real TGS guest (extraction/transcripts_raw/TGS06HermanDalyTranscript.docx.pdf) but is NOT yet modeled in this graph — a real, well-evidenced future bootstrap candidate surfaced as a byproduct of this one, not yet acted on. NO owl:sameAs asserted: checked for a genuine Wikipedia page across two separate searches 2026-07-14, none found despite extensive independent academic documentation (UVM's own faculty profile, Google Scholar citation record, ResearchGate, a full CV) — confirmed absent, not merely unchecked. Wikidata was NOT independently checked."@en ;
    thinkr:memberOf tgs:AcademicInstitution.UniversityOfVermont ;
    .

#############################################
# BOOTSTRAPPED 2026-07-15 — Gaya Herrington, first real end-to-end
# application of the corrected Stage 2 (Persona-centered) extraction
# design against a live guest, not just an eval-harness metric. Unlike
# Berman/Farley, the "stub Human, defer verification to Pass 2"
# convention effectively collapsed into a single pass here — real
# WebSearch/WebFetch verification was done in the same session as the
# initial mint, not deferred. See docs/sidecar-cleanup-handoff.md for
# the fuller trail and this methodological observation written up in
# full.
#############################################

tgs:Human.GayaHerrington a thinkr:Human, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Gaya Herrington"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Née Branderhorst (born 1981) — publicly known as 'Gaya Branderhorst' as recently as 2015-2016 (per contemporaneous coverage of Stop Straatintimidatie's founding), 'Gaya Herrington' in all TGS-era and current professional material; the Branderhorst-to-Herrington transition itself was not independently dated, flagged as a real, minor gap rather than guessed. Sustainability researcher and former financial risk consultant. Education: MSc Econometrics, Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam; MSc Sustainability Studies, Harvard University (the degree itself stays prose-only, not modeled as an AcademicInstitution Relationship — same 'current-affiliation-gets-structured, degree-history-stays-prose' precedent as tgs:Human.JoshFarley; her CURRENT Harvard teaching role is separately modeled, see tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_HarvardUniversity). Career history: De Nederlandsche Bank (the Netherlands' central bank, past role) and KPMG US (past, Director of Sustainability Services) — both kept as prose only, not modeled as Organization individuals, since neither is central to her current public Persona and both predate her most TGS-relevant work; Schneider Electric (Vice-President of Sustainability Research, 12 years advising multinationals — modeled, see tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_SchneiderElectric) was originally recorded here as current but CORRECTED 2026-07-15 via a direct WebFetch of her own bio site (gayaherrington.com/about): this is now a PAST role. As of 2026 she works as an independent advisor; also Bioregional Finance co-Director of 'Cascadia' (org not independently verified/modeled — likely 'Regenerate Cascadia' per a LinkedIn hit, not confirmed against a primary source, deliberately left unmodeled rather than guessed) and Research Collaborator with York University's Ecological Footprint Initiative (also not independently verified further, prose-only). Current Board member, Post Carbon Institute (tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_PostCarbonInstitute — notably, an ALREADY-MODELED Organization in this graph that Nate Hagens also sits on the Board of, a real cross-connection between two bootstrapped guests) and R3.0 (not independently verified/modeled, prose-only). Current Adjunct Professor, Harvard University, teaching 'Economic transformation for post-growth flourishing' (tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_HarvardUniversity); also teaches an MBA class in post-growth economics/collapse resilience at Dutch college De Veranderschool and guest-lectures at Berkeley, Cambridge, UCLA, and Shizenkan University (all prose-only, incidental rather than central). Confirmed member, Club of Rome (tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_ClubOfRome) — the organization that originally commissioned the 1972 Limits to Growth study she later empirically re-tested, the single most thematically on-point affiliation surfaced in this bootstrap. Best known for 'Update to limits to growth: Comparing the World3 model with empirical data' (Journal of Industrial Ecology, 2021, DOI 10.1111/jiec.13084) — a direct empirical re-test of the original 1972 Limits to Growth study's World3 model against 2020-era real-world data, finding current trends track most closely with the 'Business as Usual 2' and 'Comprehensive Technology' scenarios (see tgs:Work.UpdateToLimitsToGrowth2021 and tgs:LinkNote.LimitsToGrowth_GayaHerrington). Also authored the book 'Five Insights for Avoiding Global Collapse' (tgs:Work.FiveInsightsForAvoidingGlobalCollapse) and has delivered a TED talk (not independently verified/linked). Founder (2014) of Stop Straatintimidatie, a Dutch anti-street-harassment advocacy organization that became a formal nonprofit foundation in 2016 (see tgs:Organization.StopStraatintimidatie and tgs:Relationship.GayaHerrington_StopStraatintimidatie) — central to her current public Persona as a women's-rights activist, not merely an incidental past fact, hence structured rather than left as prose. Real Wikipedia page, Wikidata entry (Q110607845), AND DBpedia page all confirmed via WebSearch/WebFetch 2026-07-15 — see tgs:CrosswalkNote.GayaHerrington_Wikidata and tgs:CrosswalkNote.GayaHerrington_DBpedia. A one-year-old daughter is mentioned in the TGS_57 transcript itself but deliberately excluded here as a privacy-sensitive personal detail not central to her public Persona, consistent with this project's scope (publications/credentials/public role, not general biography) — a judgment call, not a strict rule, flagged in case a future session weighs it differently."@en ;
    thinkr:memberOf 
        tgs:Organization.SchneiderElectric, 
        tgs:Organization.StopStraatintimidatie, 
        tgs:Organization.PostCarbonInstitute, 
        tgs:Organization.ClubOfRome, 
        tgs:AcademicInstitution.HarvardUniversity ;
    .

